.TH MACME 1
.SH NAME
macme, apdf, aimg, aweb, apascdb \- acme with image windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macme
[
.I acme options
]
.PP
.B apdf
[
.B -r
.I dpi
]
.I file
[
.I page
]
.PP
.B aimg
[
.B -s
.I percent
]
.I file
.PP
.B aweb
[
.B -l
]
[
.B -h
.I height
]
.I url
.PP
.B apascdb
[
.B -g
] [
.B -L
] [
.B -w
]
.I prog.p
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Macme
is
.IR acme (1)
with one addition, as a prototype:
the body of a window can show an image instead of text.
Everything else, including its file system
(it is posted as
.B acme
too, so
.IR win ,
.IR 9p (1)
and other clients work unchanged), is the same as in
.IR acme .
.PP
Each window directory has a file
.BR image .
An
.IR image (6),
compressed or not, written to it is shown in the body
when the file is closed, replacing any previous one.
Reading it gives four numbers: the width and height of the
part of the body that shows the image, and those of the image,
so that clients can fit the image to the window.
The ctl message
.B noimage
goes back to showing text.
The ctl message
.B pagekeys
makes the PgUp, PgDn, Home and End keys come as
.B KX
events, as if
.BR pgup ,
.BR pgdown ,
.B home
or
.B end
had been executed, instead of scrolling.
.PP
The ctl message
.B place
.I n
.I percent
moves the window to the
.IR n th
visible column (counting from 0, or from the right if
.I n
is negative),
.I percent
of the way down (at the end if it is negative).
A window already in that column keeps its place among the others,
and the border above it moves to that height.
Clients use it to lay out several windows.
.PP
The wheel, the scroll bar, and the arrow, page, Home and End keys
scroll the image; typing does not change it.
On macOS, horizontal swipes (e.g., on a Magic Mouse) scroll it
sideways: macme sets
.B $hwheel
when it starts
.IR devdraw ,
which then sends them as mouse events with buttons 8 and 16
both set, button 1 set for left, and the amount shifted left 5 bits.
Clicking on the image sends an event
.IP
.EX
MI\fIx y\fP 0 1 \fIb\fP
.EE
.PP
to the window's
.B event
file, with
.I x
and
.I y
the position in the image and
.I b
the buttons (1, 2, 4), as the event text, so that
.IR acmeevent (1)
reports it as the text.
.PP
.I Apdf
shows a page of a PDF
.I file
(the first by default) in a new window,
using
.IR mutool ,
.IR pdftoppm ,
or
.IR gs ,
whichever is found first, and
.IR png (1)
to make the image
.RI ( mutool
gives the best pages; on macOS,
.BR "brew install mupdf-tools" ).
Unless
.B -r
gives the resolution, the page is fit to the window.
.B Next
and
.B Prev
in the tag, or the PgDn and PgUp keys, move through the pages;
Home and End go to the first and last ones.
.B Zoom+
and
.B Zoom-
change their size, and
.B Zoom!
fits the page to the window.
.PP
.I Aimg
shows an image file (PNG, JPEG and GIF with the
.IR jpg (1)
programs, others with ImageMagick's
.IR convert ),
with the same
.BR Zoom+ ,
.B Zoom-
and
.B Zoom!
in the tag.
.PP
.I Aweb
shows a web page (a file name or a URL) rendered as an image,
as wide as the window and
.I height
pixels tall (4000 by default), with a headless Chrome or Chromium
(or
.B $browser
if set), or Firefox.
.B Links
opens a window with the links in the page, to be plumbed;
clicking on a link with button 1 or 3 follows it, and button 2
opens it in a new window (the link boxes come from running a
script in the page; without Chrome, links are not clickable);
button 2 on a URL in the tag shows it;
.B Back
goes back,
.B Reload
renders again,
.B More
doubles the height, and
.B Zoom+
and
.B Zoom-
scale the page.
Chrome is driven through its DevTools protocol by
.IR awebcdp ,
which loads the page itself (with its scripts and redirections)
and keeps the cookies in
.BR $home/lib/aweb/cookies ,
so logins last.
Each window keeps its Chrome running
.RB ( awebcdp
.BR -S ),
so following a link does not wait for one to start;
it quits after five idle minutes.
The Chrome window opened by
.B Login
must be quit (not just closed) once logged in: while it runs,
starting Chrome on macOS only brings that one forward.
Pages cannot be typed into: to log in to a site,
.B Login
(or option
.BR -l )
opens the page in a Chrome window, where the cookies are saved
until that Chrome is quit; then
.B Reload
shows the page logged in.
.PP
.I Apascdb
runs a program of the
.I pasc
Pascal compiler under
.IR pascdb ,
its step-by-step viewer, with each of its views
in a window instead of a browser page.
It starts
.B pascdb
.B --no-browser
(or
.B $pascdb
if set; the options are passed to it)
and drives it through its HTTP interface.
The source window
.RB ( prog.p+pascdb )
shows the state in its first line, the line about to run marked with
.BR > ,
and breakpoints marked with
.BR * ;
button 2 on a line number, or
.B Break
with the line selected, toggles a breakpoint.
The data window
.RB ( +data )
shows the active calls, the global variables and the blocks
allocated with
.BR new ;
button 3 on a pointer's address goes to its block.
The stack
.RB ( +stack )
and memory
.RB ( +mem )
windows show the stack frames slot by slot and a hex dump
of the memory in use, with
.B <-fp
at the frame pointer.
The machine window
.RB ( +machine )
shows the registers of pasc's machine
.RB ( pc ,
.BR fp ,
.BR sp ),
the operand stack, top first, and the bytecode, one instruction
per row with its address, its bytes (the opcode, then the argument),
and its source line;
.B >
marks the instruction to run, with the registers repeated next to it,
and
.B -
the other instructions of its line.
The output window
.RB ( +out )
shows what the program writes; lines typed at its end are its input,
and
.B EOF
closes it.
.B Files
in the source tag opens a window with the input files and their
read positions;
.BR Data ,
.BR Stack ,
.BR Mem ,
.B Machine
and
.B Out
open those windows again after
.BR Del .
Every tag has
.BR Step ,
.B Stepi
(run one machine instruction;
.B Step
goes back to whole lines),
.BR Play ,
.BR Pause ,
.B Cont
(run to the next breakpoint),
.B Restart
and
.BR Quit ;
.B Speed
.I seconds
sets the delay of
.BR Play .
After each step the windows show the first line that changed, selected.
In
.IR macme ,
the source, machine and output windows go to the second column from the right,
and the others to the rightmost one.
.B Pict
in the data, stack, memory or files window shows that view as the
browser page draws it (boxes, and arrows for pointers and var
parameters) in the window image, rendered at each step by a headless
Chrome or Chromium (or
.BR $browser );
.B Text
goes back to text.
Deleting the source window, or
.BR Quit ,
ends
.I pascdb
and removes all the windows.
.PP
All of them write
.B dumpdir
and
.B dump
to the window's
.B ctl
file (see
.IR acme (4)),
with the page, resolution or scale in use, so
.B Dump
and
.B Load
(or a restart with
.BR -l )
bring their windows back as they were, in the same
column and place.
Other clients should do the same, as
.I win
does.
.SH EXAMPLE
Show an image computed by a program:
.IP
.EX
\&. $PLAN9/lib/acme.rc
newwindow
winname /tmp/plot
plot ... | topng | png -t | winwrite image
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B \*9/src/cmd/macme
.br
.B \*9/bin/apdf
.br
.B \*9/bin/aimg
.br
.B \*9/bin/aweb
.br
.B \*9/bin/awebcdp
.br
.B \*9/bin/apascdb
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR acme (4),
.IR acmeevent (1),
.IR jpg (1),
.IR image (6)
.br
.I https://github.com/fjballest/pasc
for
.I pasc
and
.I pascdb
.SH BUGS
It is a prototype: the image is kept in memory, the body text
is hidden but kept, and there is no way to zoom in macme itself.
.I Apdf
needs
.IR pdfinfo ,
.IR mutool ,
or
.I gs
to find the last page for End.
.PP
.I Apascdb
pictures take about a second each, and do not mark what changed;
only the source window is brought back by
.BR Load .
